.Dd July 30, 2026
.Dt CLOVE-API 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm clove-api
.Nd local HTTP API served by cloved
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr cloved 8
serves a small HTTP/1.1 API on a local unix socket
.Pq see Xr clove.conf 5 .
.Xr clove 1
is its only client today; this page documents the interface so that other
tools, and a future web interface, can use it directly.
.Pp
The API is local-only.
It is not, and will not be, compatible with the Transmission or qBittorrent
APIs.
.Ss Conventions
Every path is versioned under
.Pa /v1/ .
Replies are JSON with
.Sy Content-Type: application/json .
One request and one reply per connection: there is no keep-alive and no
chunked transfer encoding.
.Pp
Every request must carry the API token in an
.Sy X-Clove-Token
header, including over the unix socket.
The token is the contents of
.Pa <data_dir>/token ,
which the daemon writes mode 0600 on first start.
A missing or wrong token is answered with
.Sy 401 ,
compared in constant time.
.Pp
Wherever a path takes an
.Ar info-hash ,
it also takes any prefix of one that is unique among the hosted torrents, of
at least four hex characters.
A full 40-character hash is matched exactly and never treated as a prefix, so
it resolves without consulting the torrent table and cannot come to mean a
different torrent as others are added.
An ambiguous prefix is a
.Sy 409
and is never resolved by choosing one.
.Pp
An error reply is a JSON object with an
.Sy error
key and a status that says what went wrong:
.Bl -tag -width "409" -compact -offset indent
.It Sy 400
Malformed request, an unusable torrent reference, or an invalid body.
A
.Sy Content-Length
above 2 MiB is refused here, on the header, before the body is read.
Because those bytes are never drained, a client that sends the oversized body
anyway sees the connection reset rather than this reply \(em check the length
before sending.
.It Sy 401
Missing or invalid token.
.It Sy 404
No such resource or torrent.
.It Sy 405
Method not allowed for that path.
.It Sy 409
The torrent is already added, or the given prefix matched more than one
torrent.
The second case carries a
.Sy candidates
array beside the
.Sy error
key, each entry an
.Sy info_hash
and a
.Sy name ,
so a client can say which torrents were meant without asking for the listing
again.
.It Sy 500
The daemon failed the operation; the message says how.
.El
.Ss Endpoints
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy GET Pa /v1/status
Daemon and router state:
.Sy version ,
.Sy uptime_secs ,
.Sy sam_address ,
.Sy torrents ,
.Sy up_rate
and
.Sy down_rate
.Pq client-wide, bytes per second ,
.Sy peers
and
.Sy peer_limit
.Pq attached across every torrent, and the ceiling they come from ,
and
.Sy router
.Pq Sy connecting , Sy waiting-for-router , Sy connected , Sy unsupported-sam-address .
.It Sy GET Pa /v1/torrents
An array of torrent summaries, in the order the torrents were added:
.Sy info_hash ,
.Sy name ,
.Sy size ,
.Sy pieces ,
.Sy have ,
.Sy progress
.Pq 0.0 to 1.0 ,
.Sy uploaded ,
.Sy downloaded ,
.Sy up_rate
and
.Sy down_rate
.Pq bytes per second, smoothed ,
.Sy added
.Pq milliseconds since the Unix epoch ,
.Sy peers
.Pq connected right now ,
.Sy priorities ,
and
.Sy state .
.Pp
.Sy state
is one of
.Sy downloading ,
.Sy seeding ,
.Sy complete
.Pq complete but offline ,
.Sy paused ,
.Sy queued
.Pq wanted, but past Ic max_active_downloads No or Ic max_active_seeds ,
.Sy waiting-for-router ,
.Sy verifying
.Pq its data is being hashed ,
or
.Sy fetching-metadata
.Pq a magnet whose info dictionary has not arrived yet .
.It Sy POST Pa /v1/torrents Ns Op ?paused=1&sequential=1
Add a torrent.
.Sy paused=1
adds it stopped;
.Sy sequential=1
picks pieces in file order from the outset.
Both are ignored for a magnet, whose settings are applied when its metadata
arrives and it becomes an ordinary torrent.
The body is either the raw bytes of a
.Pa .torrent
file or a
.Ar magnet:\&
URI.
Answers
.Sy 201
with the
.Sy info_hash ;
a magnet also reports
.Sy state: fetching-metadata .
.It Sy GET Pa /v1/torrents/ Ns Ar info-hash
One torrent in detail: the summary fields plus
.Sy private ,
.Sy known_peers
.Pq destinations we could dial ,
.Sy pex_peers
.Pq of those, how many were first learned from a peer's Sy i2p_pex No message ,
.Sy inbound_peers
.Pq peers that dialed us rather than the other way round ,
.Sy announces_ok ,
.Sy announces_failed ,
.Sy last_announce_error
.Pq present only when an announce has failed ,
a
.Sy files
array
.Pq Sy path , Sy length , Sy priority ,
and
.Sy trackers .
.Pp
.Sy pex_peers
and
.Sy inbound_peers
count what has happened since this torrent's engine started, not what is true
now: a peer that connected and left still proves peer exchange worked and that
the inbound path carried a stream.
Both reset when the engine is restarted, and neither is persisted.
.It Sy DELETE Pa /v1/torrents/ Ns Ar info-hash Ns Op ?data=1
Remove a torrent.
With
.Sy data=1
its downloaded files are deleted too.
.It Sy POST Pa /v1/torrents/ Ns Ar info-hash Ns Pa /pause
.It Sy POST Pa /v1/torrents/ Ns Ar info-hash Ns Pa /resume
Take the torrent offline or bring it back.
Pausing disconnects its peers and persists across restarts.
A resumed torrent returns to its place in the queue, which may be
.Sy queued
rather than running.
.It Sy PUT Pa /v1/torrents/ Ns Ar info-hash Ns Pa /seed-ratio
Set this torrent's seeding ratio.
The body is a ratio written as a decimal
.Pq Sy 2 , Sy 1.75 ,
to at most three places, or
.Sy 0
to follow the daemon's
.Ic seed_ratio .
Reported as
.Sy seed_ratio
in thousandths, beside the torrent's current
.Sy ratio .
.It Sy POST Pa /v1/torrents/ Ns Ar info-hash Ns Pa /start
Resume the torrent and move it to the head of the queue.
At most one torrent is at the head; the position is not persisted.
.It Sy POST Pa /v1/torrents/ Ns Ar info-hash Ns Pa /verify
Re-check every piece against the metainfo hashes and answer with the
.Sy verified
count.
The torrent must be paused first;
.Sy 400
otherwise.
.Pp
Hashing a large torrent takes as long as reading it does, and this request
waits for it — but nothing else does: the torrent reports
.Sy verifying
to
.Sy GET Pa /v1/torrents
throughout, and every other request is answered as usual.
A second verify for the same torrent while one is running is
.Sy 400 ,
and the torrent cannot be resumed until it finishes.
.It Sy POST Pa /v1/torrents/ Ns Ar info-hash Ns Pa /peers
Hand a running torrent a peer to dial.
The body is a b32 address, with or without the
.Pa .b32.i2p
suffix.
.It Sy PUT Pa /v1/torrents/ Ns Ar info-hash Ns Pa /priorities
Set per-file priorities.
The body is a comma-separated list, one value per file in the torrent's own
order:
.Sy 0
skip,
.Sy 1
normal,
.Sy 2
high.
The count must equal the file count.
.It Sy POST Pa /v1/torrents/ Ns Ar info-hash Ns Pa /announce
Announce to every tracker now, ignoring the intervals they gave us.
Refused with
.Sy 400
if the torrent is paused, has no running engine, has no I2P trackers, or was
already forced within the last minute.
.It Sy PUT Pa /v1/torrents/ Ns Ar info-hash Ns Pa /sequential
Switch piece selection between file order and rarest-first.
The body is exactly
.Sy true
or
.Sy false ;
anything else is a
.Sy 400 .
The setting is persisted and reported as
.Sy sequential
by
.Sy GET Pa /v1/torrents/ Ns Ar info-hash .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Query status with
.Xr curl 1 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ tok=$(cat ~/.local/share/clove/token)
$ curl -s --unix-socket $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/clove.sock \e
    -H "X-Clove-Token: $tok" http://clove/v1/status
{"version":"0.1.0","uptime_secs":842,"sam_address":"127.0.0.1:7656",
 "torrents":3,"router":"connected"}
.Ed
.Pp
Add a torrent file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ curl -s --unix-socket $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/clove.sock \e
    -H "X-Clove-Token: $tok" --data-binary @release.torrent \e
    http://clove/v1/torrents
{"info_hash":"58e2fc46a8dc57c78191f079648750b0644d03a2"}
.Ed
.Pp
Add a magnet:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ curl -s --unix-socket $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/clove.sock \e
    -H "X-Clove-Token: $tok" \e
    --data-binary 'magnet:?xt=urn:btih:58e2fc46a8dc57c78191f079648750b0644d03a2' \e
    http://clove/v1/torrents
{"info_hash":"58e2fc46a8dc57c78191f079648750b0644d03a2",
 "state":"fetching-metadata"}
.Ed
.Pp
Skip the first file of a three-file torrent:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ curl -s -X PUT --unix-socket $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/clove.sock \e
    -H "X-Clove-Token: $tok" --data-binary '0,1,1' \e
    http://clove/v1/torrents/58e2fc46a8dc57c78191f079648750b0644d03a2/priorities
{"ok":true}
.Ed
.Pp
Remove a torrent and its data:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ curl -s -X DELETE --unix-socket $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/clove.sock \e
    -H "X-Clove-Token: $tok" \e
    'http://clove/v1/torrents/58e2fc46a8dc57c78191f079648750b0644d03a2?data=1'
{"ok":true}
.Ed
.Pp
A missing token is refused:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ curl -s --unix-socket $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/clove.sock http://clove/v1/status
{"error":"missing or invalid API token"}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr clove 1 ,
.Xr clove.conf 5 ,
.Xr cloved 8
.Sh CAVEATS
The API is versioned under
.Pa /v1/
and will stay compatible within that prefix, but it is young: expect fields
to be added.
Clients should ignore unknown fields rather than reject them.
